# forge-sandbox-runner@.service — keeps the factory's two services running
#   inside one repository's Docker Sandbox
#
# WHAT: Rich's rule (2026-09-07): nothing the factory runs on a repository runs
#   on the host. So the build runner (the autobuild runner, `langgraph dev`)
#   and the deploy sidecar for a repository run INSIDE that repository's
#   sandbox, on the factory's own clone of it. This unit holds one session
#   open in the named sandbox running deploy/sandbox-runner.sh, the shared
#   bootstrap in the repository's deploy/ folder, which makes the venv from
#   the read-only forge and guardkit mounts and then starts and supervises
#   the two services. The session is the unit's whole job — exactly as the
#   keeper beside it holds `sleep infinity` open — so the sandbox stays awake
#   and the services stay up for as long as the unit runs.
#
#   It is a TEMPLATE unit: the part after the '@' is the sandbox's name, so one
#   file serves every repository.
#
#       systemctl --user start forge-sandbox-runner@api-test-deploy
#       systemctl --user stop  forge-sandbox-runner@api-test-deploy
#
#   Starting it is what the repository's own deploy wrapper does, every deploy,
#   when the profile's sandbox block names the two service ports; starting an
#   already-running unit does nothing. Stopping it runs the ExecStop below,
#   which reaches inside the sandbox and ends the bootstrap and the two
#   services, and then ends the held session on the host. See WHY ExecStop.
#
#   forge-prod reaches the two services over the ports the sandbox publishes
#   to the host's loopback (the profile's sidecar_publish and runner_publish,
#   for example 127.0.0.1:8925 -> 8125 and 127.0.0.1:8924 -> 8124). They are
#   bound on every interface inside the sandbox, and on nothing but loopback
#   outside it.
#
# SCOPE: a systemd USER unit, exactly like the keeper and the host runner
#   sidecar beside it. It holds a session; it never creates, removes or
#   reconfigures a sandbox, and it runs nothing of the repository on the host.
#   Installed once by hand, attended:
#
#       mkdir -p ~/.config/systemd/user
#       cp ops/systemd/forge-sandbox-runner@.service ~/.config/systemd/user/
#       systemctl --user daemon-reload
#
#   There is nothing to enable: the wrapper starts an instance when it needs
#   one. Installing this file is an attended step and is NOT done by any build.
#
# BEFORE IT CAN WORK: the sandbox daemon must be running for this user
#   (`sbx daemon start -d --policy balanced`), Rich's Docker sign-in must have
#   been done once on this box, and the sandbox must have been created with
#   the factory's mounts (deploy/sandbox-deploy.sh does that on the first
#   deploy after the profile names the two service ports). See ops/README.md
#   and ops/systemd/README.md.
#
# WHY Restart=always: the services inside must come back whether the session
#   ended cleanly or not — a sandbox that went to sleep, a daemon restart, an
#   exec that was cut off. The bootstrap is safe to run again: the venv and the
#   install are made once and reused.
#
# WHY ExecStop (added 2026-09-11): this unit runs a CLIENT on the host and all
#   the work happens inside the sandbox. Ending the client ends nothing in
#   there: the bootstrap deploy/sandbox-runner.sh keeps running inside the
#   sandbox and keeps supervising and restarting the deploy sidecar and the
#   build runner. Without an ExecStop, therefore, "systemctl --user stop" (and
#   every "restart", which is a stop then a start) LOOKS like it worked and
#   leaves the old work running, so each restart adds another supervisor.
#
#   What that cost, on 2026-09-11: four supervisors had piled up inside the
#   api-test-deploy sandbox, the oldest a day old, all fighting for ports 8124
#   and 8125; the one holding the ports was older than the code that had just
#   been installed beneath it, so it served a mixture of old and new code, and
#   a feature build died one second after Rich tapped its gate. Nothing
#   anywhere reported a problem.
#
#   So the stop goes through the SAME door as the start — `sbx exec %i`, the
#   same sandbox instance — and asks the repository's own bootstrap to stop
#   itself: `deploy/sandbox-runner.sh stop`. That is the bootstrap's documented
#   stop mode (added in the lane beside this one): it ends the running
#   bootstrap and the services it started, inside the sandbox, and it exits 0
#   when there is nothing to stop. The unit calls that one word and knows
#   nothing about how the bootstrap names its own children — no process
#   patterns live in this file.
#
#   The leading '-' on the ExecStop line means a failing stop never fails the
#   unit: stopping something already stopped, or a sandbox that is asleep or
#   gone, is ordinary and must not leave the unit in a failed state.
#
# WHY TimeoutStopSec=60: the stop now does real work inside the sandbox — open
#   a session, end the bootstrap, wait for both services — so it needs longer
#   than the old 20 seconds, and it stays bounded so a stop that hangs cannot
#   hang the box. When the time runs out systemd kills the session as before.
#
# WHY KillMode=process: after the ExecStop above has emptied the sandbox, the
#   only thing left to kill on the host is the held session itself. The
#   sandbox's own contents are not this unit's business to kill from out here.

[Unit]
Description=Keeps the factory's deploy sidecar and build runner up inside the %i Docker Sandbox
Documentation=file:///home/richardwoollcott/Projects/appmilla_github/forge/ops/systemd/README.md
Documentation=file:///home/richardwoollcott/Projects/appmilla_github/ai-transition/docs/rewrite-on-refusal-spec-2026-09-06.md

[Service]
Type=simple
# The same PATH the keeper and the deploy sidecar unit carry, so sbx and the
# tools it reaches for are found under systemd's otherwise minimal environment.
Environment=PATH=%h/.agentecflow/bin:%h/.local/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
ExecStart=/usr/bin/sbx exec %i deploy/sandbox-runner.sh
# The stop reaches inside the same sandbox and asks the bootstrap to stop
# itself; '-' so an already-stopped unit stops cleanly. See WHY ExecStop above.
ExecStop=-/usr/bin/sbx exec %i deploy/sandbox-runner.sh stop
Restart=always
RestartSec=5
TimeoutStopSec=60
KillMode=process
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
